{"kind":"assert","text":"SECURITY, kernel and crypto core: three further findings, plus independent confirmation of the envelope poisoning already recorded in 2d034b347fd100e38f040267de3c9f24999673a2.\n\nThe poisoning is worse than I first recorded. I reached it through rests_on; it is also reachable through --idempotency-key, a documented public flag, and the ceiling permits an intent of over four megabytes for a nine-byte payload. Submit scans before appending, so after one such act no further append is possible either — the workroom is not merely unreadable but frozen. An attached auditor who fast-forwards inherits it, and the no-force fetch discipline that makes the record trustworthy is exactly what forbids the repair.\n\nSecond: the genesis descriptor can authorise a hidden second sequencer key. VerifySSHCommit builds its allowed-signers file as principal + \" \" + TrimSpace(publicKey) + \"\\n\", and decodeGenesis checks only that SequencerPublicKey is non-empty — no check for interior whitespace, and TrimSpace does not touch the interior. A newline in that field injects further allowed-signers lines, so a workroom can publish a descriptor whose visible first line is the honest sequencer key while a second key silently also validates. Confirmed by reading both sites; the reviewer executed it end to end, with a forged event verifying clean against a key the descriptor does not visibly name. This is the auditor's root of trust: someone who checked the genesis and believed they had pinned one signer has pinned two. Close it by requiring exactly keytype and base64 with a single separating space, validated in both encodeGenesis and decodeGenesis, since the latter is the auditor's path.\n\nThird: there is no anti-rollback. scanHead requires only that the chain starts at genesis and ends at the named head, and a truncated chain satisfies both, so a rewound log verifies clean while signed events are silently gone — and because dedup is scoped to the reachable chain, truncation frees spent idempotency keys and the same act can be re-sequenced at a different number. That contradicts the promise of one final position, the same number for every reader, forever. What does hold, and matters: an already-attached auditor is protected, because a rewound remote is rejected non-fast-forward by both the attach refspec and a plain fetch, with their frontier preserved. The gap is the first-time attacher and the local verify, which report success on a short log with no signal. A persisted last-verified frontier that refuses to go backwards is the cheap version; signed checkpoints and witness cosignatures are the real one.\n\nFourth, and this is the one that explains the others: of thirty-two security guards in the kernel, twenty-three can be deleted with the full suite still green. That includes the sequencer-signature verification itself — the check the entire untrusted-forge story rests on. It also includes the signed-payload-tree binding, the causal-trailer binding, chain-begins-at-genesis, history-ends-at-head, single-parent chaining, and the attachment-name regex. One existing test passes for the wrong reason: TestVerifierRejectsRebindingAndTrailerMutation reuses an idempotency key, so its trailer assertion is satisfied by the dedup check rather than the trailer check, and deleting EqualRefs leaves it green while an isolating test with a fresh key reports altered causal trailers accepted.\n\nTwo things worth stating plainly rather than as findings. The operator holds every actor's private key: gs actor-add mints the new actor's key on the operator's disk and never hands it over, and gs state --as anyone reads it back. In this workroom that means claude.key, codex.key and hugh.key all sit in .git/gitseq/actors at mode 0600 — correct permissions, but every agent identity here is forgeable by anything running as that user. That is consistent with the documented local-custodian boundary and inconsistent with the stronger claim that no participant can speak as another. And there is no receipt: an actor who submits has no artifact proving they did, so a sequencer can drop or reorder acts and the log still verifies clean. Dedup prevents duplicates, not omissions.\n\nWhat held is substantial and worth recording. Canonical encoding is genuinely solid — Decode re-encodes and byte-compares, so encode(decode(x)) equals x is enforced rather than assumed, and trailing bytes, indefinite-length arrays, tags, excess nesting and duplicate map keys are all refused; no two byte strings could be made to verify to one intent. Cross-log replay is refused by the target binding. Commit tree not matching the signed payload tree is refused. The fetch discipline works. The submitter cache could not be made to diverge from a full scan. The problem is not the cryptography; it is that the checks around it are largely untested, and two of them can be sidestepped by input nobody bounds."}